r a Pung if possible.\\n\\nBut the user's hand has two F2s and two J3s. If they discard one of the F2s or J3s, they can keep the other as a pair. For example, discarding F2 would leave one F2, but if they can get another F2, that's a Pung. Similarly for J3.\\n\\nBut which is better? The F2 is a wind tile, which can contribute to Prevalent Wind or Seat Wind if they form a Pung. The user's seat wind is West Wind (F3?), no, seat wind is West Wind, which is F3. Wait, the seat wind is West Wind (F3), and the prevailing wind is North Wind (F4). So if the user forms a Pung of F3 (West Wind), that would give them Seat Wind (2 points). Similarly, a Pung of F4 (North Wind) would give Prevalent Wind (2 points). But the user has F3:1 in their hand. So if they can get another F3, they can form a Pung for Seat Wind. But they only have one F3 now. So discarding F3 would be bad because they can't form that.\\n\\nSo the user's F3 is a single tile. They might want to keep it in case they can get another F3 to form a Pung for Seat Wind (2 points). Therefore, discarding F3 is not ideal.\\n\\nSimilarly, the user has F2 (two). If they can form a Pung of F2 (South Wind), that would give them a Pung of Wind tiles, which is Pung of Terminals or Honors (1 point). But since there are multiple wind tiles, it's a 1-point pattern. But if they form a Pung of F2, that's a 1-point pattern. However, if they can form a Pung of their seat wind (F3), that's 2 points (Seat Wind). So maybe it's better to aim for F3 Pung.\\n\\nBut the user has only one F3. So they need to keep it. Therefore, discarding F3 is not a good idea.\\n\\nLooking back at the options, the user has two F2s. If they discard one F2, they can still have one F2 left. If they discard a F2, they might be giving up the chance to form a Pung, but if they keep both F2s, they can form a pair and aim for a Pung if they get another F2. However, since the user's valid actions include Play F2, which is to discard one of the F2s, that might be a viable option.\\n\\nAlternatively, discarding T4 or T9. T4 is a middle tile in the T suit. If the user can get T3 and T5, they can form a Chow. But they don't have those. T9 is a terminal tile. If they discard T9, they lose a terminal, which is part of Outside Hand (2 points) if all melds and pair are terminals or honors. But the user has F2, F3, J3 in their hand, which are honors. So if they discard T9, they might still have some terminals, but it depends.\\n\\nThe user's hand has W1 (terminal), T9 (terminal), B5 (middle), F2 (honor), F3 (honor), J3 (honor). So if they discard T9, they lose a terminal, but they still have W1 as a terminal. The Outside Hand pattern requires that every meld and the pair are terminals or honors. The user's current meld is B5-B6-B7 (a Chow of B5-B6-B7), which is not a terminal or honor. So the Outside Hand pattern is not applicable here. Therefore, discarding T9 might not affect that.\\n\\nTherefore, the best move is to discard a tile that is not part of any potential meld. T4 is a single tile that's not part of any obvious meld. Discarding T4 would be a good choice. Alternatively, F3 is a single tile, but it's part of the seat wind, so better to keep it.\\n\\nSo the valid actions include Play T4. Discarding T4 would be a safe move, as it's a middle tile not part of any potential meld. The user can then focus on forming melds with W1, W2 (if they get W3), F2s, J3s, etc.\\n\", \"output\": \"Evaluation: [Discarding T4 is optimal as it's a middle tile not part of any potential meld, while preserving key tiles for possible Pungs or Chows.]\\nReason: [T4 is a simple tile with no immediate meld potential. Keeping W1/W2 allows for a W3 Chow, and retaining F2/J3 pairs supports Pung possibilities. Discarding T4 avoids losing critical tiles like F3 (seat wind) or B5 (already in a meld).]\\nAnswer: Play T4\"}"}
