e three left. \n\nWhich is the safest? \n\nIf I discard W4, it's less likely to be needed by others. \n\nAlternatively, if I discard B5, but I have another B5. \n\nHmm. Let's think about the scoring patterns. \n\nMy current hand has a chow of B5-B6-B7. The rest are two pairs (W1, W2) and some singles. \n\nTo form a winning hand, I need two more melds. \n\nIf I can form two more chows or pungs. \n\nFor example, if I have W1, W2, and get a W3, that's a chow. \n\nOr if I have B3 and get a B4, B3-B4-B5. \n\nOr if I have B8 and B9, but need B7 again. \n\nSo the best way is to try to form another chow. \n\nIf I discard B5, I can still have one B5. Maybe that's not helpful. \n\nAlternatively, if I discard W4, which is a single tile. \n\nBut which tile is more likely to help me form a meld? \n\nIf I keep B3, and if I can get a B4, that would help. So maybe don't discard B3. \n\nSimilarly, keeping W1 and W2 as pairs. \n\nSo the least useful tile is W4. \n\nTherefore, the best action is to play W4. \n\nBut wait, the valid actions include Play W4. \n\nSo the answer is to play W4.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The current hand has a chow of B5-B6-B7 and two pairs (W1, W2). The key is to discard a tile that doesn't hinder potential melds. W4 is a single tile not part of any sequence or pair, making it the safest discard.\nReason: Discarding W4 avoids giving others a chance to form a useful meld and maintains the possibility of forming additional chows (e.g., W1-W2-W3 or B3-B4-B5) with remaining tiles.\nAnswer: Play W4"}
